<!-- AREAS -->
<section style="padding:76px 24px 60px;background:#fff;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;text-align:center;">
    <span class="kicker">Service Areas</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3vw,36px);margin:0 0 16px;">Serving Manitoba and Saskatchewan</h2>
    <div class="rich" style="text-align:left;"><p>We cover homes in <a href="/radon-winnipeg/">Winnipeg</a> and <a href="/radon-brandon/">Brandon</a> in Manitoba, and <a href="/radon-regina/">Regina</a>, <a href="/radon-saskatoon/">Saskatoon</a>, <a href="/radon-moose-jaw/">Moose Jaw</a> and <a href="/radon-prince-albert/">Prince Albert</a> in Saskatchewan, plus the towns around them. Of the homes tested in the 2024 Cross-Canada survey, 46.6% in Regina and 37.4% in Winnipeg were at or above 200 Bq/m3, and Saskatoon came in lowest at roughly 1 in 6 tested homes. The same survey found no part of Canada is radon-free, so every town on this list is worth a test.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ -->
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EEF2F7;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Do I really need to test before I get a quote?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">Yes. Health Canada's guideline is based on the average level over time, so it recommends a long-term test of at least 91 days in the heating season. Short tests and digital monitors are good for screening, but they can't tell you whether a system is worth paying for.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">How is a mitigation job priced and scoped?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">A certified mitigation professional visits the house and looks at the slab, any sump pit or crawl space, and where the pipe and fan can run and vent. Winnipeg installers advertise systems from about $1,500 to $3,000, and a Regina installer quotes $2,500 to $3,500, adding that crawl spaces or multiple suction points can cost much more. Your own price comes only from the written quote after that visit.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">How long does it take to get someone out, and how long does the work take?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">We don't publish a set response time, because the professionals are independent and their calendars vary; they'll give you a date when you call. The test itself takes at least 91 days, and Health Canada says a system can usually be installed in less than a day. One Winnipeg installer, Radon Manitoba, adds 20% to its prices for off-season work between November 30 and May 20, so ask about timing when you get your quote.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Is 200 Bq/m3 a legal limit?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">No. Bq/m3 means becquerels per cubic metre, and 200 Bq/m3 is Health Canada's guideline (about 5.4 pCi/L, the US unit). No law in Manitoba or Saskatchewan requires homeowners to test or mitigate. Above 200, Health Canada recommends fixing it within a year, and it also says no level is considered risk free.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Is there any help paying for a system?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">The Canadian Lung Association's Lungs Matter program offers up to $1,500 for lower- and moderate-income households, and it needs a C-NRPP long-term test over 200 and a C-NRPP quote before work starts. In Manitoba, the Manitoba Hydro Home Energy Efficiency Loan covers radon mitigation up to $5,000 over 5 years at 6.2% (O.A.C.). In Saskatchewan, Lung Saskatchewan says radon mitigation counts as an eligible expense for the Home Renovation Tax Credit; confirm that with CRA before you count on it.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Is Prairie Radon Co a certified radon company?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">No. We're an advertising service that connects homeowners with independent C-NRPP certified professionals, and we don't test or install anything ourselves. Certification in Canada is voluntary and belongs to the individual professional, so check their name in C-NRPP's online directory before you hire them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

